There are constants $\delta>0$, $C<\infty$, $\gamma>0$, and $M_0<\infty$ such that, for every $M\ge M_0$, every $i\in\mathbb{Z}$, and every $n\in\mathbb{N}$, $$\label{can:event-bound}
\mathbb{P}\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
\min\bigl(\log_M\left\lVert T_{i,i-n}\right\rVert,
          \log_M\left\lVert T_{i,i+n}\right\rVert\bigr)\ge(1-\delta)n,\\[2pt]
\log_M\left\lVert T_{i-n,i+n}\right\rVert\le\delta n
\end{array}
\right\}
\le C\exp(-\gamma n).$$ All four constants are independent of the center, the length, and the parameter $M$ in the indicated range.

For every $\eta>0$ there are $\eta'>0$ and $M_\eta<\infty$ such that the following holds for $M\ge M_\eta$. Fix $q_e$, a base coordinate $\theta_*$, and indices $j_+,j_-\ge2$ on the two outward paths. Suppose both indices are good at $\theta_*$ for a common normalized solution, and their logarithms satisfy <a href="#can:matching-condition" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="can:matching-condition">[can:matching-condition]</a>. Let $$L_+=\left\lvert t_{j_+}^+(\theta_*)\right\rvert,\qquad
L_-=\left\lvert t_{j_-}^-(\theta_*)\right\rvert,\qquad L=\max(L_+,L_-).$$ Let $\mathcal S\subset[-1,1]$ consist of the $h$ for which, at $\theta=\theta_*+h/L$, the same two indices are good for some common solution with $u_e=1$. The solution is allowed to depend on $h$, and neither endpoint smallness nor a new matching condition is required at these other points. Then $$\label{can:scarcity-bound}
\left\lvert\{x\in\mathbb{R}:\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,\mathcal S)<\eta'\}\right\rvert\le\eta.$$ Here and below the size of a subset of the line or circle denotes its Lebesgue measure. The constants are uniform over the central position, the base coordinate, and the selected indices.

Fix a base index $i$ and write $$D_j=\left\lVert T_{i,j}\right\rVert,\qquad
 R_{jp}=M^{g(j,p)-g(i,j)-g(i,p)}.$$ There is an absolute constant $C_0\ge1$ such that, for every orbit, every sufficiently large parameter, and every choice of integer indices, $$\label{scale:R-fourpoint}
 R_{jp}\le C_0\max\{R_{jl},R_{lp}\}.$$

Fix integers $i$, $n\ge1$, and $1\le s\le n$, and put $$L=i-n,\quad R=i+n,\quad L'=i-s,\quad R'=i+s,$$ $$D=n-\min\{g(i,L),g(i,R)\},\qquad
 \ell=g(i,L)+g(i,R)-g(L,R).$$ Use the quantities $R_{uv}$ of <a href="#scale:fourpoint" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="scale:fourpoint">11</a> with base $i$. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 g(i,L'),g(i,R')&\ge s-D,
 \label{scale:cut-wings}\\
 R_{L'R'}&\le C_0^2\max\{R_{LR},M^{-2s+2D}\}.
 \label{scale:cut-R}
\end{aligned}$$ There is a constant $A$ independent of the parameter and indices such that the cut cancellation loss satisfies $$\label{scale:cut-loss}
 g(i,L')+g(i,R')-g(L',R')
 \ge\min\{\ell,2s-2D\}-A,$$ and consequently $$\label{scale:cut-full}
 g(L',R')\le2s-\min\{\ell,2s-2D\}+A.$$

Along the assumed zero-exponent parameter sequence, there are powers of two $n_0=n_0(K)$ and $N=N(K)>n_0$, and numbers $\epsilon=e_N$, such that $$\label{scale:selected-limits}
 n_0\to\infty,\qquad \epsilon\to0,\qquad
 \epsilon n_0\to\infty,$$ and, for every fixed integer $m\in\mathbb{Z}$ (once the lengths below are positive integers), $$\label{scale:selected-boundaries}
 e_{2^m n_0}=o(\epsilon),\qquad
 e_{2^m N}=O_m(\epsilon).$$ All scale sums below run over powers of two.

Let $I$ have dyadic endpoints and length $p=2^m$, where $m\in\mathbb{Z}$ is fixed. Then $$\label{scale:sum-test-bound}
 \int\bigl(J(I)+V(I)\bigr)\,\,\mathrm{d}\mu_K=O_I(1).$$ Uniformly over the individual dyadic scales $n_0\le n\le N$, $$\label{scale:individual-test-bound}
 \epsilon^{-1}\mathbb{E}\bigl[h(I)(g_n)+J(I)(g_n)+V(I)(g_n)\bigr]
 =O_I(1).$$ At the initial scale the stronger bound holds: $$\label{scale:initial-test-bound}
 \epsilon^{-1}\mathbb{E}\bigl[h(I)(g_{n_0})+J(I)(g_{n_0})
                         +V(I)(g_{n_0})\bigr]=o(1).$$ All three statements concern sufficiently large $K$, so that the indicated endpoints and half-endpoints align.

As $I$ ranges over intervals with dyadic endpoints and power-of-two length, the common zero set of $J(I)+V(I)$ is exactly $\mathcal A$. Consequently, every compact subset $F\subset\mathcal U$ admits finitely many such intervals $I_1,\ldots,I_b$ and a number $c_F>0$ such that $$\label{scale:finite-test-cover}
 \sum_{a=1}^{b}\bigl(J(I_a)+V(I_a)\bigr)\ge c_F
 \quad\hbox{on }F.$$

After passing to a subsequence, the restrictions of $\mu_K$ and $\nu_K$ to $\mathcal U$ converge vaguely to locally finite nonnegative Borel measures $\mu$ and $\nu$, respectively. Moreover, $$\label{scale:initial-negligible}
 \xi_K\longrightarrow0\quad\hbox{vaguely on }\mathcal U.$$ The measures $\mu$ and $\nu$ are supported on the closed class $X_0\subset X$ consisting of arrays satisfying $d(s,t)\le|s-t|$ and the inequalities $$\label{scale:tree-inequality}
 B_v(s,t)\ge\min\{B_v(s,p),B_v(p,t)\},\qquad
 B_v(s,t)=d(v,s)+d(v,t)-d(s,t)$$ for every $v,s,t,p\in\mathcal D$. Every member of $X_0$ extends uniquely to a continuous semimetric on $\mathbb{R}$, obeying the same inequalities at all real arguments. Both limiting measures are invariant under $$(\tau_r d)(s,t)=d(s+r,t+r)\qquad(r\in\mathcal D).$$ For every interval $I$ as in <a href="#scale:local-mass" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="scale:local-mass">15</a>, $$\label{scale:limiting-test-bound}
 \int(J(I)+V(I))\,\,\mathrm{d}\mu<\infty,
 \qquad
 \int(h(I)+J(I)+V(I))\,\,\mathrm{d}\nu<\infty.$$ In particular, $$\label{scale:terminal-shortfall}
 \int h([0,1])\,\,\mathrm{d}\nu\le1.$$

On $\mathcal U$, with the measures supported on $X_0$ as above, $$\label{scale:measure-balance}
 S_*\mu=\mu+\nu.$$ There is a locally finite nonnegative measure $\mu_\infty$, invariant under $S$ and every dyadic translation, such that $$\label{scale:measure-decomposition}
 \mu=\mu_\infty+\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}(S^{-j})_*\nu,
 \qquad S_*\mu_\infty=\mu_\infty.$$ The series is a series of positive measures dominated by $\mu$.

Suppose there are two exterior unit rays with slow interval $[a,b]$, and put $L=b-a$. Then $L>0$, and, with the fixed constant $$\label{shape:cross-constant}
 C_*=2+\frac6\delta,$$ every $z\in\mathbb{R}$ and $t>0$ satisfy $$\label{shape:cross-bound}
 0\le 1-\frac{d(z,z+t)}t\le\frac{C_*L}{t}.$$ The shortfall is zero whenever the interval is wholly in either closed unit ray.

